analysis:
Both changed files are CI test scripts for the review tooling (.github/scripts/test-threat-bedrock.py and test-threat-budget.py). They cover no provider, coordinator or native-runtime code, so none of the threat-model assets (A-001..A-017) or boundaries (TB-002/003/007/009/010/011) are directly affected.

test-threat-bedrock.py: adds tests asserting that Bedrock requests now carry outputConfig.textFormat.structure.jsonSchema built by threat_review.structured_output.bedrock_schema. It also asserts that SOL gets additionalModelRequestFields strict=True, that local constraints such as maxLength move into the schema description, and that the original SCHEMA is not mutated. The production implementation (bedrock.py, structured_output.py) is not in the supplied evidence, so it is unverified here.

test-threat-budget.py: adds a mock GitHub contents service with branch-level 409 conflicts and tests for these behaviors:
- retry of a report checkpoint is bounded at 8 attempts;
- unknown failures such as timeouts are not retried;
- exhausted ledger conflicts raise BudgetStopped and leave the ledger unchanged, so spending is not admitted;
- the cache is create-only and does not overwrite an existing entry;
- the canonical threat model is read from a pinned base-tree blob (docs/threat-model.yaml, sha e*40/f*40). This is a positive control: a PR cannot alter its own review context.
- diagnostics stay content-free.
The run() signature drops its root argument, which is consistent with reading from the pinned blob.

The tests show fail-closed budget and clearance behavior, with no regressions visible in these test files. The corresponding non-test source changes (state.py conflict_backoff, budget_runner, bedrock, structured_output) were not supplied, so the integration pass should check them if they are in the PR: the retry bounds, whether ledger writes keep their CAS semantics, and whether the pinned base blob is read from the base SHA and not the head. No findings.
